  "summary": "Wide Kermani wool panels embroidered edge-to-edge with silk paisleys and tree-of-life scenes.",
  "description": "Pateh is Kerman's grand textile: broad widths of hand-woven wool (ariz) completely covered by double-crewel silk stitches — no ground left bare — forming paisley boteh borders, central cypress trees and narrative scenes in deep madders, greens and blues. Traditionally made by women working collectively for dowries and ceremonial gift cloth, pateh absorbed carpet composition at fabric scale. Twentieth-century revivals turned famous panels into museum pieces; workshops in Kerman still teach the stitch.",
